1
00:01:45,551 --> 00:01:56,751
<v AMBER>  Hello William. This is Amber – you said to phone if I wanted to get more information about the job agency you mentioned. Is now a good time?


2
00:01:56,751 --> 00:02:08,677
<v WILLIAM>        Oh, hi Amber. Yes. Fine. So the agency I was talking about is called Bankside – they’re based in Docklands – I can tell you the address now – 497 Eastside.


3
00:02:08,677 --> 00:02:14,591
<v AMBER>   OK, thanks. So is there anyone in particular I should speak to there?


4
00:02:14,591 --> 00:02:18,480
<v WILLIAM>      The agent I always deal with is called Becky Jamieson.


5
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:21,532
<v AMBER>  Let me write that down – Becky …


6
00:02:21,532 --> 00:02:29,487
<v WILLIAM>        Jamieson J-A-M-I-E-S-O-N.


7
00:02:29,487 --> 00:02:32,812
<v AMBER> Do you have her direct line?


8
00:02:32,812 --> 00:02:50,104
<v WILLIAM>       Yes, it’s in my contacts somewhere – right, here we are: 078 double 6, 510 triple 3. I wouldn’t call her until the afternoon if I were you – she’s always really busy in the morning trying to fill last-minute vacancies.

9
00:02:50,104 --> 00:02:56,894
<v WILLIAM>   She’s really helpful and friendly so I’m sure it would be worth getting in touch with her for an informal chat.


10
00:02:56,894 --> 00:03:00,883
<v AMBER>  It’s mainly clerical and admin jobs they deal with, isn’t it?


11
00:03:00,883 --> 00:03:13,849
<v WILLIAM>      That’s right. I know you’re hoping to find a full-time job in the media eventually – but Becky mostly recruits temporary staff for the finance sector – which will look good on your CV – and generally pays better too.


12
00:03:13,849 --> 00:03:13,949
<v AMBER>    Yeah – I’m just a bit worried because I don’t have much office experience.


13
00:03:20,046 --> 00:03:40,949
<v WILLIAM>      I wouldn’t worry. They’ll probably start you as a receptionist, or something like that. So what’s important for that kind of job isn’t so much having business skills or knowing lots of different computer systems – it’s communication that really matters – so you’d be fine there. And you’ll pick up office skills really quickly on the job. It’s not that complicated.


14
00:03:40,949 --> 00:03:50,479
<v AMBER> OK good. So how long do people generally need temporary staff for? It would be great if I could get something lasting at least a month.


15
00:03:50,479 --> 00:04:00,192
<v WILLIAM>       That shouldn’t be too difficult. But you’re more likely to be offered something for a week at first, which might get extended. It’s unusual to be sent somewhere for just a day or two.


16
00:04:00,192 --> 00:04:07,041
<v AMBER>  Right, I’ve heard the pay isn’t too bad – better than working in a shop or a restaurant.


17
00:04:07,041 --> 00:04:11,993
<v WILLIAM>     Oh yes – definitely. The hourly rate is about £10, 11 if you’re lucky.


18
00:04:11,993 --> 00:04:18,033
<v AMBER>  That’s pretty good. I was only expecting to get eight or nine pounds an hour.

19
00:04:59,928 --> 00:05:02,474
<v WILLIAM>        Do you want me to tell you anything about the registration process?


20
00:05:02,474 --> 00:05:06,468
<v AMBER>   Yes, please. I know you have to have an interview.


21
00:05:06,468 --> 00:05:11,162
<v WILLIAM>      The interview usually takes about an hour and you should arrange that about a week in advance.


22
00:05:11,162 --> 00:05:18,726
<v AMBER>   I suppose I should dress smartly if it’s for office work – I can probably borrow a suit from Mum.


23
00:05:18,726 --> 00:05:23,061
<v WILLIAM>      Good idea. It’s better to look too smart than too casual.


24
00:05:23,061 --> 00:05:28,366
<v AMBER> Will I need to bring copies of my exam certificates or anything like that?


25
00:05:28,366 --> 00:05:30,711
<v WILLIAM>        No – they don’t need to see those, I don’t think.


26
00:05:30,711 --> 00:05:32,878
<v AMBER>   What about my passport? 

27
00:05:32,878 --> 00:05:34,998
<v WILLIAM>      Oh yes – they will ask to see that.


28
00:05:34,998 --> 00:05:36,185
<v AMBER>  OK.


29
00:05:36,185 --> 00:05:56,045
<v WILLIAM>     I wouldn’t get stressed about the interview though. It’s just a chance for them to build a relationship with you – so they can try and match you to a job which you’ll like. So there are questions about personality that they always ask candidates – fairly basic ones. And they probably won’t ask anything too difficult like what your plans are for the future.


30
00:05:56,045 --> 00:05:57,842
<v AMBER>     Hope not.


31
00:05:57,842 --> 00:06:09,501
<v WILLIAM>       Anyway, there are lots of benefits to using an agency – for example, the interview will be useful because they’ll give you feedback on your performance so you can improve next time.


32
00:06:09,501 --> 00:06:12,698
<v AMBER>   And they’ll have access to jobs which aren’t advertised.


33
00:06:12,698 --> 00:06:16,510
<v WILLIAM>        Exactly – most temporary jobs aren’t advertised.


34
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:25,958
<v AMBER>   And I expect finding a temporary job this way takes a lot less time – it’s much easier than ringing up individual companies.


35
00:06:25,958 --> 00:06:29,003
<v WILLIAM>       Yes indeed. Well I think …